Using intelligibility and accountability goals, this research proposes an interpretable and user-centric method to explanation provision in autonomous driving. It builds on risk object identiﬁcation technique in driving scene [7] and trafﬁc objects representation using scene graphs [8] (see Figure 1). The observations recorded in the scene graphs, the AV’s actions, and the road rules are combined to generate explanations. In this paper, we only focus on how this combination can generate different explanations (i.e. the proposed treebased method), and the type of driving conditions where the explanations are mostly appropriate (i.e. through a user study).
Our main contributions are: • We propose an interpretable tree-based representation
for generating different types of explanations based on

observations, actions, and road rules. • We present a user study that evaluates different types
of explanations (generated by following the proposed tree-based approach) in a range of driving scenarios. In particular, we show the importance of causal explanations in safety-critical scenarios. • In contrast to existing work on explanations in AVs, we apply a triangulation design method (i.e. both quantitative and qualitative methods) to evaluate explanations for autonomous driving against intelligibility and accountability goals.
